  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Oklahoma federal contracts totaled $4.6B in FY2026 to date. The federal government made 19,346 awards in the state with an average award size of $237K. Obligations fell 32.6% year over year. Contracting activity has varied across recent fiscal years: FY21 totaled $4.5B, FY22 totaled $5B, FY23 totaled $4.9B, FY24 totaled $5.5B, and FY25 totaled $6.8B. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $3B in FY2026 to date. The Department of Transportation obligated $634M and the Department of Health and Human Services obligated $218M. The largest vendors in the current-year snapshot include THE BOEING COMPANY at $980M and a second listing for THE BOEING COMPANY at $179M, followed by V2X AEROSPACE LLC at $119M and MCKESSON CORPORATION at $113M. Aircraft Manufacturing is the largest industry by obligations at $836M, followed by Engineering Services at $410M and Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing at $368M. All figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contract spending in Oklahoma moved over the last five fiscal years?",
        "body": "Obligations rose from $4.5B in FY21 to $5B in FY22, then were $4.9B in FY23 and $5.5B in FY24 before peaking at $6.8B in FY25. Obligations in FY26 are $4.6B year to date, and obligations fell 32.6% year over year. The average award size in FY2026 to date is $237K across 19,346 awards."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries dominate Oklahoma federal contracting in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $3B in FY2026 to date, followed by the Department of Transportation at $634M and the Department of Health and Human Services at $218M. Aircraft Manufacturing leads industries with $836M, Engineering Services follows with $410M, and Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing is $368M. Major vendors include THE BOEING COMPANY at $980M and V2X AEROSPACE LLC at $119M."
      }
    ]
  },
